  "worked_solution": "The $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$, the intermediate value theorem states that \n\n*If $k$ is a number that lies between $f(-2)$ and $f(2)$ inclusively, then there exists a number $c\\in [-2,2]$ such that $f(c)=k.$*\n\nFrom the graph we see that $f(-2)=1$ and $f(2)=2.$Therefore, the missing part is \"$c\\in [-2,2]$\".\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062906/assets/455863da8f126505f018a356f5f89bbbea81f1e1e90e803a51390bed22514a9a.png)",
